  "ref": "2CH.23.9",
  "review_notes": [
    "Rule 49's genitive limit governs *that had been king David's*: removing *had been* removes only the English tense, while *David's* still writes the belonging relation together with the name. The competing separate לְ → *had been* reading mistakes that copular frame for a separately rendered relation. The appositional title retains its own *king*, but does not prevent the name's genitive from carrying the preposition's relation; rule 30 explicitly limits its contiguity requirement to the compound expressions discussed there, not fusion generally. Thus s022 and s025 share *had been ... David's*, headed on s025/t021, while the unrendered article and the title remain separate.",
    "*and bucklers and shields* writes one *the* for the whole coordinated object series, so rule 47 gives it to the first article and marks the other two unrepresented."
  ]
